## Intent & Decisions
Let the provider configure the prefill pool of a prefill/decode (PD)
deployment, which the inference API already supports.

Settled choices:
- An optional pd attribute holds prefill_resources and
  prefill_autoscaling (min, max, concurrency), mapped to the API
  DeploymentPD message. The existing resources and autoscaling
  attributes keep configuring the decode pool.
- A change to pd updates the deployment in place. hot_load uses
  RequiresReplace because the API treats it as immutable; pd is not.
- Create and update both send Pd, because UpdateDeployment replaces the
  whole specification. Without Pd in the update request, any update
  from Terraform would remove the prefill pool.
- prefill_autoscaling leaves out capacity_classes. An empty list gives
  the API default order, which is what the intended deployments set
  explicitly. This removed 39 lines (the ElementsAs loop, diagnostics
  plumbing, and the null-preserving list read). Trade-offs: a prefill
  capacity class set outside Terraform is cleared by the next Terraform
  update, and the prefill pool does not inherit the decode pool's
  capacity_classes. Adding the optional attribute later is a
  backward-compatible schema change.
- pdFromProto is a pure mapping from the API message: concurrency is
  null when the API returns 0. The decode autoscaling read instead
  keeps the prior model value in place. For valid configurations the
  result is the same, because concurrency has an AtLeast(1) validator,
  so 0 always means unset. The pure form needs no prior-state lookup.
- The lifecycle test reuses the hotLoadImportHandler mock with one added
  line (Pd: req.Pd). Renaming the mock was rejected to keep the diff
  small. An example resource change was dropped for the same reason.

